 ## Subtopics
 
-### [Sobel-based Gradient Estimation](9967-sobel-based-gradient-estimation.md)
-Uses fixed 3x3x3 convolution kernels. Faster but lower order accuracy. Choose for real-time performance where smoothness is less critical [G].
+### [2nd-Order Finite Difference Gradient](13052-2nd-order-finite-difference-gradient.md)
+Uses immediate neighbors with 2nd-order accuracy. Choose when computational cost is critical and high-frequency noise is not a concern.
 
-### [Forward Difference Gradient](3362-forward-difference-gradient.md)
-Uses only forward neighbors. 1st-order accurate. Simpler to implement but less smooth [G].
-
-### [Gauss-Seidel with Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR)](6904-gauss-seidel-with-successive-over-relaxation-sor.md)
-An iterative solver for linear systems, not a direct gradient estimator. Used for solving Poisson equations for SDF reconstruction [G].
-- [Jacobian Estimation via Central Differences]: Focuses on the full Jacobian matrix rather than just the gradient vector. Useful for deformation analysis [G].
+### [Central Difference Stencils](9968-central-difference-stencils.md)
+General central difference methods. The 4th-order method is a specific, higher-accuracy variant of central differencing with specialized boundary handling.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N = N_x N_y N_z$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel is accessed a constant number of times [G].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the input grid and $O(N)$ for the output gradients. Temporary arrays for concatenation add $O(N)$ overhead [G].
-- **Measured Performance**: Performance depends on the implementation and hardware acceleration [G].
-- **Strengths**: High-order accuracy (4th-order) provides very smooth normals, reducing artifacts in ray marching. Maintains accuracy at boundaries [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Requires at least 3 voxels per dimension. Boundary extrapolation can be sensitive to noise [G]. More complex to implement than simple central differences [G].
-- **When to Use**: When high-quality normals are critical for rendering fidelity, such as in high-resolution SDF rendering or neural rendering pipelines [G].
-- **When Not to Use**: For very low-resolution grids (< 3 voxels) or when computational resources are extremely constrained and lower-order accuracy is acceptable [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y N_z)$, as it requires a single pass over the grid for each axis. The constant factor is higher than 2nd-order due to the wider stencil and boundary logic, but still linear.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for the input grid and $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ for the output gradient grids. Intermediate shifted arrays also require $O(N_x N_y N_z)$ memory.
+- **Strengths:** Provides significantly smoother normals than 2nd-order methods, which is beneficial for ray marching stability and shading quality in SDF rendering. Maintains high accuracy even at boundaries.
+- **Limitations:** Requires more memory for intermediate buffers. Sensitive to noise in the input grid; smoothing may be required before gradient computation. Requires $N \ge 3$.
+- **When to use:** When rendering SDFs or smooth scalar fields where normal quality is paramount and computational resources allow for the extra memory and arithmetic.
+- **When not to use:** For very small grids ($N < 3$) or when memory is extremely constrained. For noisy data, pre-filtering is needed.
 
 ## Background
-The method is implemented in a PyTorch-based repository for differentiable rendering of Signed Distance Functions, where smooth gradients are necessary for stable optimization and high-quality visualization [S1, unofficial]. The 4th-order boundary extrapolation formula is a standard technique in numerical analysis for maintaining high-order accuracy in finite difference schemes on bounded domains [G].
+The method is implemented in the context of differentiable rendering of Signed Distance Functions, specifically in the `SDFDiff` framework [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the property that 4th-order finite differences provide a better approximation of the derivative for smooth fields, reducing aliasing artifacts in the rendered normals.
 
